          "line": 313,
          "text": "2.2 Git Basics - Recording Changes to the Repository"
        },
        {
          "line": 314,
          "text": "Recording Changes to the Repository"
        },
        {
          "line": 317,
          "text": "Git repository on your local machine, and a checkout or"
        },
        {
          "line": 320,
          "text": "Typically, you’ll want to start making changes and committing snapshots of those changes into your repository each time the project reaches a state you want to record."
        },
        {
          "line": 326,
          "text": "Tracked files are files that were in the last snapshot, as well as any newly staged files; they can be unmodified, modified, or staged."
        },
        {
          "line": 329,
          "text": "When you first clone a repository, all of your files will be tracked and unmodified because Git just checked them out and you haven’t edited anything."
        },
        {
          "line": 330,
          "text": "As you edit files, Git sees them as modified, because you’ve changed them since your last commit."
        },
        {
          "line": 331,
          "text": "As you work, you selectively stage these modified files and then commit all those staged changes, and the cycle repeats."
        }
      ]
    },
